# Line endings, fixed rather than left to whoever checked out.
#
# `install.sh` shipped with CRLF. Git's `core.autocrlf` converted it on a
# Windows checkout, `Dockerfile.web` copied the working tree verbatim into the
# image, and `curl -fsSL https://thetabase.co/install.sh | sh` then failed on
# every Linux machine with `sh: : not found` -- because a POSIX shell reads the
# carriage return as part of the command name.
#
# Nothing caught it. The script's syntax is valid, `sh -n` passes on Git Bash,
# and the only test that could ever have seen it is running the published
# installer on a real Linux box, which is what finally did.
#
# So: text files get LF in the repository and LF on checkout, everywhere.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Things that must be LF to function at all, stated explicitly so a future
# `* text=auto` change cannot quietly take them with it.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.mk        text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Windows-only, and genuinely wants CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Binary, so Git must not touch the bytes.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.tar.gz    binary
*.wasm      binary
*.capnp-bin binary
